<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Galindians</h1><p class="st-meta">100 CE – 1283 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southernmost Baltic people of Prussia, living among the Masurian lakes and named by Ptolemy, whose land was almost empty by the time the Teutonic Knights arrived.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ptolemy listed the Galindai in the 2nd century, and they are usually located in the lake country of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, roughly modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masuria">Masuria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Like their neighbours the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sudovians">Sudovians</a> and other Prussian groups, they lived in scattered clans around fortified hilltops, without any known central authority.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Almost nothing is recorded of their institutions. They farmed, fished and hunted in a land of forest and lakes, and shared the pagan religion and West Baltic speech of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Prussians</a>. The Teutonic chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-dusburg">Peter of Dusburg</a> claimed that Galindia had been depopulated by wars and famine long before his time.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the late 13th century the area formed part of the great wilderness that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> absorbed during its conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, completed in 1283. Survivors were merged into the Prussian population, and the region was later resettled by German and Polish (Masurian) colonists.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/galindians.jpg" alt="Galindians" width="320" height="353"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baltic_Tribes_c_1200.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 CE – 1283 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="